## Solution

**Setup.** The four men are named Baker (B), Fisher (F), Forester (F), Miller (M), with professions drawn from the same set, each man holding exactly one distinct profession. Mr. X is a fifth man whose name equals his brother's name (his brother being one of the four men).

**Condition (3) on the four men.** Since Fisher and Forester both have initial **F**, the men named Fisher and Forester cannot hold a profession starting with F, so each must hold Baker or Miller. Being distinct, one gets Baker and the other Miller (2 ways). The remaining professions {Fisher, Forester} go to the men named Baker and Miller (2 ways). This yields exactly **4 permutations**:

| Case | Baker→ | Fisher→ | Forester→ | Miller→ |
|------|--------|---------|-----------|---------|
| 1a | Fisher | Baker | Miller | Forester |
| 1b | Forester | Baker | Miller | Fisher |
| 2a | Fisher | Miller | Baker | Forester |
| 2b | Forester | Miller | Baker | Fisher |

**Determining P_X (Mr. X's profession).** By condition (1), P_X = profession of the man named Baker:
- Cases 1a, 2a: P_X = **Fisher** (initial F).
- Cases 1b, 2b: P_X = **Forester** (initial F).

In every case P_X starts with **F**.

**Determining the brother.** Mr. X's name = brother's name, so condition (3) for Mr. X requires the brother's name-initial ≠ F. This eliminates the men named Fisher and Forester. The brother is either the man named Baker or the man named Miller.

- **Brother = Baker(man):** his profession = P_X (since P_X is defined as Baker's profession), violating condition (2) (P_X ≠ P_B). ✗ in all 4 cases.
- **Brother = Miller(man):** his profession is Forester (cases 1a, 2a) or Fisher (cases 1b, 2b), which differs from P_X. ✓ Also Mr. X's name = Miller (initial M) ≠ F = P_X's initial. ✓

So in **all four valid configurations**, the brother is the man named **Miller**, and Mr. X is named **Miller**.

**Counting.**

- **n₁ (possible names for Mr. X):** only Miller → **n₁ = 1**.
- **n₂ (possible professions for Mr. X):** Fisher or Forester → **n₂ = 2**.
- **n₃ (possible pairs (P_X, P_B)):**
  - (Fisher, Forester) — cases 1a, 2a.
  - (Forester, Fisher) — cases 1b, 2b.
  
  → **n₃ = 2**.

**Result.**

$$n_1 \times n_2 \times n_3 = 1 \times 2 \times 2 = \boxed{4}$$

